1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:06,030
Okay in Avatar when they have sex in Pandora

2
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:08,630
they hook up their ponytails, so we know their ponytails

3
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:11,220
are like their junk.

4
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:12,850
Yeah... so?

5
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:15,020
So, when they ride horses and fly on the birds,

6
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:16,390
they also use their ponytails.

7
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:17,590
What's your point?

8
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:19,480
My point is, if I were a horse or a bird,

9
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:22,230
I'd be very nervous around James Cameron.

10
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,500
It amazes me how you constantly obsess over fictional details

11
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:30,470
when there are more important things in the real world to worry about.

12
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:34,040
For example, why wasn't William Shatner in the new Star Trek movie?

13
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:37,410
Hey, Sheldon. I was up in the administration office,

14
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,110
and I happened to overhear the name of the winner

15
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:42,480
of this year's Chancellor's Award for Science.

16
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:44,550
And you want to rub my nose in the fact

17
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:46,420
that my contributions are being overlooked again?

18
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:49,120
I am the William Shatner of theoretical physics.

19
00:00:50,490 --> 00:00:51,520
All right, I'll play.

20
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:54,690
What self-important, preening fraud are they honoring this year?

21
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:57,260
Oh, I'm so glad you asked it like that. You.

22
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,300
- I won?  - You won.

23
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:03,740
I won!

24
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:05,540
This is astonishing.

25
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:08,140
Not that I won the award-- no one deserves it more.

26
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:11,680
Actually, I guess I misspoke. It's not astonishing; more like inevitable.

27
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:15,250
I'm not sure what to do first. Maybe I should call my mother.

28
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:19,960
Wait! I know-- I'm going to conduct an interview with myself and post it online.

29
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:24,150
Well, good for him.

30
00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:27,060
Yeah, the one thing the William Shatner

31
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:31,160
of theoretical physics needed was an ego boost.

32
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:17,630
Didn't it look like that spear was going to go right through your skull?

33
00:02:17,670 --> 00:02:19,100
No.

34
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:24,090
Hey, you didn't want a Slurpee at 7-Eleven, you don't get glasses.

35
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:29,980
Oh, that will be another congratulatory call for me.

36
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:31,710
Uh, mute, please.

37
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:33,950
Wait, wait. Hang on-- flaming arrow.

38
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:38,650
Hello?

39
00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:41,490
Oh, Chancellor Morton, how are you, sir?

40
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:46,630
Yes, I was expecting your call. Three years ago.

41
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,160
I see.

42
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,170
Wait. What happens if I choose not to give a speech?

43
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:53,840
Uh-huh.

44
00:02:53,870 --> 00:02:56,470
And if I don't want to forfeit the award?

45
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:00,540
Well, you've got that tied up in a neat little bow.

46
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:01,610
All right.

47
00:03:01,650 --> 00:03:03,210
Thank you.

48
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:05,080
Problem.

49
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:06,550
What?

50
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:09,280
They expect me to give a speech at the banquet.

51
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:10,890
I can't give a speech.

52
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:15,790
Well, no, you're mistaken. You give speeches all the time.

53
00:03:17,150 --> 00:03:20,960
What you can't do is shut up.

54
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:23,030
Yeah, before the movie,

55
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:25,460
you did 20 minutes on why guacamole turns brown.

56
00:03:26,570 --> 00:03:28,470
It turned brown while you were talking.

57
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:32,470
I am perfectly comfortable speaking to small groups.

58
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:34,640
I cannot speak to large crowds.

59
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:36,150
What, to you, is a large crowd?

60
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:38,650
Any group big enough to trample me to death.

61
00:03:40,020 --> 00:03:44,050
General rule of thumb is 36 adults or 70 children.

62
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:47,090
Sheldon, congratulations.

63
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:48,760
Brought you a cheesecake from work.

64
00:03:48,790 --> 00:03:50,260
You know, 'cause of your award,

65
00:03:50,290 --> 00:03:52,090
not because a busboy sneezed on it.

66
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,930
- I'm not accepting the award.  - Why not?

67
00:03:57,970 --> 00:04:01,470
Turns out the great Sheldon Cooper has stage fright.

68
00:04:01,470 --> 00:04:02,640
That's no reason to back out.

69
00:04:02,870 --> 00:04:05,640
You know, I once got a pretty big honor in high school,

70
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:08,080
and I was terrified about appearing in front of a big crowd,

71
00:04:08,110 --> 00:04:10,280
but I went through with it, and you know what?

72
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:12,180
The world looked pretty darn good

73
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:14,880
sitting on a haystack in the back of a Ford F-150

74
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:17,950
as a member of the Corn Queen's court.

75
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:20,990
Thank you.

76
00:04:21,020 --> 00:04:22,320
Yeah, I'll bear that in mind

77
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:24,990
if I'm ever nominated for the Hillbilly Peace Prize.

78
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:29,600
Sheldon, you're being ridiculous.

79
00:04:29,630 --> 00:04:30,630
Am I?

80
00:04:30,670 --> 00:04:31,970
Let me tell you a story.

81
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,670
Where's 70 children when you need them?

82
00:04:35,870 --> 00:04:40,510
I was 14 and graduating summa cum laude from college.

83
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:43,850
"Summa cum laude" is Latin for "with highest honors."

84
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:48,520
I just love how you always skip over the part where no one asks.

85
00:04:48,550 --> 00:04:51,020
I was valedictorian and expected to give an address.

86
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:53,290
Even now, I can remember that moment

87
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:56,490
when I walked up to the podium and looked out at the crowd.

88
00:04:56,530 --> 00:04:58,490
There must have been thousands of people.

89
00:04:58,530 --> 00:05:00,730
My heart started pounding in my chest.

90
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:02,300
I began to hyperventilate.

91
00:05:02,330 --> 00:05:05,970
My vision became blurry, and before I knew it...

92
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:07,940
Oh, dear.

93
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:09,540
Oh, my God.

94
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:10,610
Sheldon?

95
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:12,170
Sheldon, are you okay?

96
00:05:12,210 --> 00:05:14,210
Don't trample me.

97
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:17,610
Come on, Mother.

98
00:05:17,650 --> 00:05:20,650
You know why I can't accept the award.

99
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:24,850
With all due respect, I don't think praying will help.

100
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:28,960
No, I have not heard the song, "Jesus, Take the Wheel."

101
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:32,960
No, no... You don't need to start singing it.

102
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,160
Yes, I'll buy it on the iTunes, Mother.

103
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:37,800
Good-bye, Mother.

104
00:05:43,470 --> 00:05:45,610
Hello.

105
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,940
Sit down. We want to talk to you.

106
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:49,280
Am I in trouble?

107
00:05:49,310 --> 00:05:51,180
Did my mother call you?

108
00:05:51,210 --> 00:05:52,580
Just sit.

109
00:05:52,620 --> 00:05:56,250
We think we can help you with your stage fright.

110
00:05:56,290 --> 00:05:57,890
Oh, I doubt that.

111
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:01,090
I haven't figured out a way, and I'm much smarter than all of you.

112
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:06,400
Yes, but you're not smarter than all of us put together.

113
00:06:06,430 --> 00:06:09,500
Oh, I'm sorry. That is what I meant.

114
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:13,870
Okay. Your problem is, you're trying to do this all by yourself.

115
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:15,900
We can help you. We can be your team.

116
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:18,740
Like, uh, Professor Xavier and his X-Men.

117
00:06:22,350 --> 00:06:25,110
I do like the X-Men.

118
00:06:25,150 --> 00:06:27,350
Did I see X-Men?

119
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:29,990
Yeah, we watched it last week. You said you liked it.

120
00:06:30,020 --> 00:06:32,720
Oh. I say a lot of things, sweetie.

121
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:35,590
So, how about it, Sheldon?

122
00:06:35,630 --> 00:06:37,060
I don't know.

123
00:06:37,090 --> 00:06:40,560
If you're my X-Men, what are your powers?

124
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:44,400
Okay. Well, I am going to take you shopping, get you a nice suit.

125
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:45,800
Might give you more confidence.

126
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:48,000
That's not exactly a mutation that would get you

127
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:50,380
into Xavier's School for Gifted Youngsters,

128
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:50,940
but go on.

129
00:06:50,970 --> 00:06:52,240
Leonard?

130
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:54,310
I thought I could try to analyze you

131
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:56,350
and get to the root of your anxiety.

132
00:06:56,380 --> 00:06:59,720
What qualifies you to attempt to understand my mind?

133
00:06:59,750 --> 00:07:02,280
My mother is a highly regarded psychiatrist,

134
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:05,550
and I've been in therapy ever since she accused me

135
00:07:05,590 --> 00:07:08,090
of breast-feeding codependently.

136
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:12,430
Raj says he can teach you...

137
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:14,430
What did you call it?

138
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:17,730
I don't know-- some Indian meditation crap.

139
00:07:19,270 --> 00:07:24,050
I see. Well, I assume, since the rest of you have set the bar so low,

140
00:07:24,050 --> 00:07:26,440
you're saving the most impressive contribution for last.

141
00:07:26,480 --> 00:07:29,880
Go ahead, Howard. Dazzle me.

142
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:34,620
My power is the ability to pretend

143
00:07:34,650 --> 00:07:38,550
like I give a damn about your piddily-ass problems.

144
00:07:43,190 --> 00:07:46,030
And that's 24-seven, buddy.

145
00:07:47,230 --> 00:07:50,170
And I appreciate the pretense.

146
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:52,570
So, what do you say, Sheldon?

147
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:54,270
Are we your X-Men?

148
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:55,470
No.

149
00:07:55,510 --> 00:07:58,870
The X-Men were named for the "X" in Charles Xavier.

150
00:07:58,910 --> 00:08:03,250
Since I am Sheldon Cooper, you will be my C-Men.

151
00:08:08,250 --> 00:08:10,420
Oh, that's not a good name.

152
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:18,770
Okay, Sheldon. I'm going to be leading you

153
00:08:18,810 --> 00:08:20,980
through a series of meditation exercises.

154
00:08:21,010 --> 00:08:24,280
These methods come from the ancient gurus of India

155
00:08:24,310 --> 00:08:27,480
and have helped me overcome my own fears.

156
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:31,320
And yet, you can't speak to women.

157
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:34,790
True, but thanks to meditation, I am able to stay

158
00:08:34,820 --> 00:08:37,280
in the same room with them without urinating.

159
00:08:39,530 --> 00:08:42,960
Now... close your eyes.

160
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,900
Okay, but don't punch me.

161
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:47,270
What?

162
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:49,540
When I was little, my sister would say to me,

163
00:08:49,570 --> 00:08:51,440
"Close your eyes, you'll get a surprise,"

164
00:08:51,470 --> 00:08:53,240
and then she'd punch me.

165
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:55,080
I'm not going to punch you.

166
00:08:55,110 --> 00:08:57,050
That's what my sister used to say.

167
00:08:57,980 --> 00:08:59,550
Do you want to do this or not?

168
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:01,350
I'm sorry. Proceed.

169
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:03,050
All right.

170
00:09:03,090 --> 00:09:06,890
Imagine yourself in the one place you feel most at home.

171
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:08,260
Where is that?

172
00:09:08,290 --> 00:09:11,230
Sim City.

173
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:15,930
More specifically, the Sim City I designed--

174
00:09:15,970 --> 00:09:18,000
Sheldonopolis.

175
00:09:18,030 --> 00:09:20,470
Okay, you're in Sheldonopolis.

176
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:22,200
Where exactly?

177
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:24,340
Sheldon Square? Sheldon Towers?

178
00:09:24,370 --> 00:09:28,010
Sheldon Stadium, home of the Fighting Sheldons?

179
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:29,510
Whatever you like.

180
00:09:29,550 --> 00:09:32,280
I thought this was supposed to be a guided meditation.

181
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:35,120
Fine. You're in Sheldon Square.

182
00:09:35,150 --> 00:09:36,320
Really? This time of year?

183
00:09:36,350 --> 00:09:37,720
It's a bit nippy.

184
00:09:37,750 --> 00:09:39,790
Then, put on a sweater.

185
00:09:39,820 --> 00:09:41,720
Suppose I could run downtown

186
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:45,030
and pick up something at Shel-Mart.

187
00:09:45,060 --> 00:09:47,000
Yeah, whatever. Just go buy a sweater.

188
00:09:47,030 --> 00:09:49,330
You know, the nice thing about Shel-Mart is I own it,

189
00:09:49,370 --> 00:09:51,770
so I get a 15% discount.

190
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:55,440
You own the damn thing. Just take a freaking sweater!

191
00:09:55,470 --> 00:09:57,640
Look, I didn't turn a profit last quarter

192
00:09:57,670 --> 00:10:00,640
by taking product off the shelves willy-nilly.

193
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:03,410
All right.

194
00:10:03,450 --> 00:10:07,450
You've paid for a sweater, and you're in Sheldon Square.

195
00:10:07,480 --> 00:10:09,850
Hang on.

196
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:13,760
It's a cardigan. I have to button it.

197
00:10:16,660 --> 00:10:18,030
Oh, no.

198
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:19,190
What now?

199
00:10:19,230 --> 00:10:22,230
A Godzilla-like monster is approaching the city.

200
00:10:22,260 --> 00:10:24,570
I have to get my people to safety.

201
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:27,770
People of Sheldonopolis, this is your mayor.

202
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:29,040
Follow me.

203
00:10:29,070 --> 00:10:32,670
If the children can't run, leave them behind.

204
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:37,550
Oh, the simulated horror!

205
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:48,590
Raj?

206
00:10:49,860 --> 00:10:51,960
Just as I suspected.

207
00:10:51,990 --> 00:10:54,600
Meditation is nothing but hokum.

208
00:11:01,170 --> 00:11:02,710
I question your premise.

209
00:11:02,750 --> 00:11:04,950
How is a new suit going to prevent me

210
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:07,250
from passing out in front of a ballroom full of people?

211
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:09,250
It'll give you confidence.

212
00:11:09,290 --> 00:11:10,950
You know, sometimes when I'm feeling all stressed out

213
00:11:10,990 --> 00:11:13,250
about something, I go out and buy a cute top

214
00:11:13,290 --> 00:11:16,390
or a fun skirt and I have a whole new outlook on life.

215
00:11:16,430 --> 00:11:20,230
Don't you eventually realize you're just the same stressed out person

216
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:22,330
in a cute top or a fun skirt?

217
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:25,030
Yeah, that's when I buy shoes.

218
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:27,700
Now, let's see what we've got.

219
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:31,270
This is nice.

220
00:11:31,310 --> 00:11:34,180
It's only one color.

221
00:11:34,210 --> 00:11:35,540
Yeah, so?

222
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:38,750
That's a lot of money for only one color.

223
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:39,780
Fine.

224
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:41,620
Why don't you pick out what you like.

225
00:11:45,050 --> 00:11:47,190
This is pretty sharp.

226
00:11:52,630 --> 00:11:55,560
No. You're wrong.

227
00:11:56,730 --> 00:11:58,670
This is great.

228
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:02,000
I had a suit like this when I was six.

229
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:07,980
Okay, I think we have a winner.

230
00:12:10,750 --> 00:12:12,550
Where the hell'd you find that?

231
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:14,920
In the prom department.

232
00:12:14,950 --> 00:12:16,320
It's ridiculous.

233
00:12:16,350 --> 00:12:20,260
Says the former member of the Corn Queen's Court.

234
00:12:21,190 --> 00:12:23,760
Please just try this one on.

235
00:12:23,790 --> 00:12:28,660
Okay. But anything I put on now is only going to suffer in comparison.

236
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:38,010
This is absurd. I look like a clown.

237
00:12:41,910 --> 00:12:44,610
So, Sheldon...

238
00:12:46,520 --> 00:12:48,350
...how you doing?

239
00:12:48,380 --> 00:12:51,990
That's how you start a psychotherapy session?

240
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:53,320
"How am I doing?"

241
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:55,820
I was promised a riverboat journey

242
00:12:55,860 --> 00:12:58,090
into the jungles of my subconscious.

243
00:12:58,130 --> 00:12:59,990
Instead, I get the same question I hear

244
00:13:00,030 --> 00:13:03,160
from the lady who slices my bologna at Ralph's.

245
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,230
I'm sorry, I'll start again.

246
00:13:06,270 --> 00:13:10,370
Would it be helpful to you if I told you about my dreams?

247
00:13:10,410 --> 00:13:12,370
Um, I don't know, maybe.

248
00:13:12,410 --> 00:13:17,180
I recently had a dream that I was a giant.

249
00:13:17,210 --> 00:13:19,750
But everything around me was to scale,

250
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:21,880
so it all looked normal.

251
00:13:25,050 --> 00:13:29,190
How did you know you were a giant if everything was to scale?

252
00:13:29,220 --> 00:13:32,760
I was wearing size a million pants.

253
00:13:35,900 --> 00:13:37,900
Why don't we just talk?

254
00:13:39,070 --> 00:13:41,540
The talking cure. Classical Freudian. Good choice.

255
00:13:41,570 --> 00:13:43,540
If it will help speed things along,

256
00:13:43,570 --> 00:13:47,080
uh, my answers to the standard Rorschach ink blot test are:

257
00:13:47,110 --> 00:13:52,080
A) a bat, B) a bat, c) a bat,

258
00:13:52,110 --> 00:13:57,520
and D) my father killing my mother with a hypodermic needle.

259
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:00,720
Why don't I just start?

260
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:05,460
Sometimes people have trouble accepting accolades

261
00:14:05,490 --> 00:14:09,330
if, on a subconscious level, they don't feel they deserve them.

262
00:14:09,370 --> 00:14:11,530
Do you think maybe that's what's happening here?

263
00:14:11,570 --> 00:14:14,100
Really, Leonard?

264
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:18,040
You're just going to try to recycle Adler's doctrine

265
00:14:18,070 --> 00:14:19,470
of the inferiority complex?

266
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:22,510
I could probably get that from the woman at Ralph's.

267
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:25,950
And she'd let me taste some pieces of cheese for free.

268
00:14:25,980 --> 00:14:27,880
But it could be part of your problem.

269
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:29,280
Let me give you an example.

270
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:30,890
When I was eight,

271
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:33,090
I won a ribbon at the science fair

272
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:36,760
for my project, "Do Lima Beans Grow Better to Classical Music."

273
00:14:36,790 --> 00:14:39,030
But my mother pointed out

274
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:41,960
that it was just a rehash of my brother's earlier

275
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,930
"Do Lima Beans Grow Worse to Rock 'n' Roll."

276
00:14:46,470 --> 00:14:50,170
I felt so guilty, I gave the ribbon back.

277
00:14:50,210 --> 00:14:52,740
And how did that make you feel?

278
00:14:52,780 --> 00:14:54,710
Terrible.

279
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:57,650
I worked really hard on that project.

280
00:14:57,680 --> 00:15:01,080
I stayed up all night singing the clown's aria from Pagliacci

281
00:15:01,120 --> 00:15:03,920
to a lima bean sprout.

282
00:15:04,850 --> 00:15:07,390
Go on.

283
00:15:07,420 --> 00:15:09,390
It wasn't my fault.

284
00:15:09,430 --> 00:15:11,990
I had never seen my brother's project.

285
00:15:12,030 --> 00:15:14,200
And my mother could've told me before

286
00:15:14,230 --> 00:15:17,430
instead of at the ceremony in front of everyone.

287
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:22,840
So, I hear you saying you're angry with your mother.

288
00:15:22,870 --> 00:15:25,940
Damn right, I'm angry with my mother.

289
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:34,120
For God's sake, I was eight years old.

290
00:15:34,150 --> 00:15:36,020
She humiliated me.

291
00:15:36,050 --> 00:15:38,920
That's when the bed-wetting started again.

292
00:15:40,860 --> 00:15:42,590
Thank you, Leonard.

293
00:15:42,620 --> 00:15:44,160
For what?

294
00:15:44,190 --> 00:15:45,960
If someone as damaged as you

295
00:15:45,990 --> 00:15:48,360
can find his way to crawl out of bed each morning,

296
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:50,870
I think I can face a simple award ceremony.

297
00:15:51,770 --> 00:15:56,370
Wait, that's it? I thought we had a whole hour!

298
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:01,840
Good evening, ladies and gentlemen. My name is Dr. Leonard Hofstadter,

299
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:03,850
and it is my very great honor to introduce

300
00:16:03,850 --> 00:16:06,750
the winner of this year's Chancellor's award for Science

301
00:16:06,780 --> 00:16:09,580
and my good friend, Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

302
00:16:10,890 --> 00:16:12,890
But before I do,

303
00:16:12,920 --> 00:16:15,320
I'd like to share with you a letter from Sheldon's mother,

304
00:16:15,320 --> 00:16:16,990
who couldn't be here tonight.

305
00:16:16,990 --> 00:16:21,660
Isn't that nice? His mother sent him a letter.

306
00:16:21,700 --> 00:16:26,570
She's proud of him. I wonder what that feels like...

307
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:34,070
"Dear Shelly." that's what she calls him. Shelly-- it's a pet name.

308
00:16:34,110 --> 00:16:37,610
You know what my mother's pet name for me is? Leonard.

309
00:16:39,850 --> 00:16:43,080
But I digress.

310
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:44,150
"Dear Shelly..."

311
00:16:44,190 --> 00:16:46,090
Oh, dear.

312
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:47,090
What's the matter?

313
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:48,290
I'm getting dizzy.

314
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:49,620
Don't worry.

315
00:16:49,660 --> 00:16:52,430
You're surrounded by your C-Men.

316
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:54,960
I can't do this.

317
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:56,360
I'm going to faint.

318
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:58,430
Here, drink this.It'll relax you.

319
00:16:58,300 --> 00:16:59,460
Alcohol?

320
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:02,270
- I don't drink alcohol. - Fine, faint.

321
00:17:06,070 --> 00:17:09,510
I don't feel different.This alcohol is defective.

322
00:17:09,540 --> 00:17:12,780
Here, see if this one works.

323
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:19,920
First of all, the projects were totally different.

324
00:17:19,950 --> 00:17:23,090
I was showing that classical music nurtures lima beans

325
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:26,460
and makes them grow, but my mother didn't hear me.

326
00:17:26,490 --> 00:17:29,300
If you'd like to look at the relationship between nurturing and growth,

327
00:17:29,300 --> 00:17:33,660
I'd like to point out that my brother is eight inches taller than me.

328
00:17:33,700 --> 00:17:35,700
I'm ready.

329
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:39,440
right.

330
00:17:39,470 --> 00:17:43,210
Ladies and gentlemen, our guest of honor Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

331
00:17:45,940 --> 00:17:49,510
Thanks, shorty, I'll take it from here.

332
00:17:49,550 --> 00:17:52,250
All right, you people ready to have some fun?

333
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:54,850
You have a basic understanding of differential calculus

334
00:17:54,890 --> 00:17:57,050
and at least one year of algebraic topology?

335
00:17:57,090 --> 00:17:58,990
Well, then here come the jokes.

336
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:04,260
Why did the chicken cross the Mobius strip?

337
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:07,200
To get to the same side-- bazinga!

338
00:18:08,370 --> 00:18:11,600
All right, a neutron walks into a bar and asks, "How much for a drink?"

339
00:18:11,640 --> 00:18:13,740
The bartender says, "For you, no charge."

340
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:20,710
Hello?

341
00:18:21,610 --> 00:18:22,710
I know you're out there.

342
00:18:22,750 --> 00:18:27,050
I can hear you metabolizing oxygen and expelling carbon dioxide.

343
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:31,190
Looks like we have some academic dignitaries in the audience.

344
00:18:31,220 --> 00:18:33,820
Dr. Randall from the geology department--

345
00:18:33,860 --> 00:18:36,560
only man who's happy when they take his work for granite.

346
00:18:36,600 --> 00:18:38,730
Ba-da cha!

347
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:42,400
I kid the geologists, of course,

348
00:18:42,430 --> 00:18:44,740
but it's only 'cause I have no respect for the field.

349
00:18:46,040 --> 00:18:49,010
Let's get serious for a moment.

350
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:55,110
Why are we all here?

351
00:18:55,150 --> 00:18:57,010
'Cause we're scientists.

352
00:18:57,050 --> 00:18:59,950
And what do scientists study?

353
00:18:59,990 --> 00:19:01,690
The universe.

354
00:19:01,720 --> 00:19:03,850
And what's the universe made of?

355
00:19:03,890 --> 00:19:06,390
I am so glad you asked.

356
00:19:06,420 --> 00:19:10,390
There's antimony, arsenic, aluminum, selenium

357
00:19:10,430 --> 00:19:12,700
And hydrogen and oxygen and nitrogen and rhenium

358
00:19:12,730 --> 00:19:15,230
And nickel, neodymium, neptunium, germanium

359
00:19:15,270 --> 00:19:16,330
Everybody!

360
00:19:16,370 --> 00:19:18,340
And iron, americium

361
00:19:18,370 --> 00:19:21,240
Ruthenium, uranium, europium, zirconium,

362
00:19:21,270 --> 00:19:22,770
Lutetium, vanadium

363
00:19:22,810 --> 00:19:23,770
Just the Asians!

364
00:19:23,810 --> 00:19:25,580
And lanthanum and osmium

365
00:19:25,610 --> 00:19:27,280
And astatine and radium...

366
00:19:43,500 --> 00:19:45,830
Penny, Leonard.

367
00:19:45,870 --> 00:19:48,300
Would you be able to answer some questions

368
00:19:48,330 --> 00:19:51,500
I'm having about the events of last night?

369
00:19:51,540 --> 00:19:53,140
Sure.

370
00:19:53,170 --> 00:19:55,270
Question one...

371
00:19:55,310 --> 00:19:57,280
where are my pants?

372
00:19:57,310 --> 00:19:59,780
You might want to check YouTube.

373
00:20:01,810 --> 00:20:03,150
What do I search?

374
00:20:03,180 --> 00:20:05,850
It's already loaded. Just hit play.

375
00:20:07,950 --> 00:20:11,660
All right, people, let's get down to the math.

376
00:20:11,690 --> 00:20:16,360
It is only three dimensional thinking that limits our imagination.

377
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:18,730
Can I take my pants off over my head?

378
00:20:18,770 --> 00:20:21,030
Of course not.

379
00:20:21,070 --> 00:20:22,600
My body's in the way.

380
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:28,810
But if we had access to higher dimensions,

381
00:20:28,840 --> 00:20:32,680
we could move our pants around our bodies

382
00:20:32,710 --> 00:20:35,910
through the fourth dimension

383
00:20:35,950 --> 00:20:39,920
and our days of dropping trousers would be over.

384
00:20:43,320 --> 00:20:45,120
Oh, Lord...

385
00:20:45,160 --> 00:20:47,660
This couldn't be any more humiliating.

386
00:20:47,690 --> 00:20:48,760
Give it a minute.

387
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:51,730
Now, for the astronomers in the audience,

388
00:20:51,730 --> 00:20:54,620
get ready to see the dark side of the moon.

389
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:58,810
And here's Uranus.

